cartridge "STAR DRAGON/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1986"

' STAR DRAGON
' A ColecoVision game based on "Star Dragon" by Sean D. Wagle, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette June 1986 p. 54 (Commodore 64 machine language "STAR", SYS 49392,
' with the 16-sprite raster routine "16" from the same article). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the article's and the original's
' (run in jsc64 and read from its machine code, orig/star.dis), rebuilt for
' the ColecoVision: tile stars for the body, a sprite head, sounds, a tune.
'
' The game: a dragon made of a trail of 15 stars slithers across the
' screen. The Dragon Gun at the bottom moves left and right and fires.
' Only a shot on the dragon's HEAD counts: that star falls away and the
' next one becomes the head. A shot that hits any other star ricochets
' down at a random slant, and a bouncing shot that reaches the gun
' destroys it; so does the dragon touching the gun. Three guns. When the
' last star of a dragon falls: dragons 1 and 2 of a level are worth 20
' points, the third 60. Ten levels, 30 dragons, 1000 points in all.
'
' From the original (orig/star.dis): the head flies in straight lines with
' a random speed per axis (0-63/256 a step, the sign random); at a wall it
' takes a new random speed whose sign points back into the field, and a
' random 0-31 x 4 steps later it turns once more to another random
' velocity; the body follows the same path; each level makes the dragon
' faster; a fire press (not a held button) fires; losing a gun starts the
' dragon over with all 15 stars; the gun is destroyed by a ricochet or by a
' star level with it. Measured in jsc64 (orig/c64speed-sdragon.mjs): the
' head covers about 2.2 C64 pixels a frame (|dx| + |dy|) at level 1, 4.3 at
' level 5, 5.5 at level 10 (the C64 game was not frame-locked: more work
' made each step slower).
'
' Liberties: the C64 drew the stars as 15 multiplexed sprites (each half
' shown every other frame, so they looked see-through). Here the body is
' made of background tiles that snap to the 8-pixel grid (no flicker on
' the 4-sprites-a-line ColecoVision), the head is a sprite, white with
' eyes, so it can be told apart. On the C64 the body stars trailed only 4
' steps apart and bunched into a clump; here they are laid every 7 pixels
' along the head's path, so the dragon is a long trail as the article
' describes. Speeds are per frame: head speed m x Spd(level) / 256 pixels
' a frame per axis, m 8-63 (the C64 allowed 0, a dragon that could hang
' still), Spd 4, 5, 7, 9, 10 at levels 1-5 (gentler than measured), up to 18 at level 10. The C64 kept
' up to 64 shots and ricochets as bitmap pixels; here 3 shots and 3
' ricochets (a shot that would ricochet with no free slot just vanishes).
' The gun moves 3 pixels a frame (the C64's moved several times faster,
' as fast as its loop ran). The dragon stops while the gun explodes. The
' C64 used + and - and CTRL, SHIFT LOCK to pause; here the joystick.
' Added so the gun can't win by standing still (a parked gun cleared
' levels in tests): when the gun has not moved for 2 seconds, a head above it within 40 pixels flies up
' and away, and a shot that ricochets comes straight back at the gun.
' After 3 seconds the dragon dives past the floor line, keeping clear of
' the gun's column, and slides slowly along the bottom into it (an idle
' player loses all three guns in about a minute).
' Added for fairness (tuned so a player reacting a quarter second late
' clears levels): the head bounces off a floor line 3 rows above the gun;
' one time in six it dives past it at half its vertical speed; ricochets
' fall 2 pixels a frame (C64 about 3); a new gun
' is safe for 1 second (drawn grey). Levels 1-5 are slower than measured.
' Added: a title page, a credits page, a title tune, sound effects, the
' "DRAGON SLAIN" pause between dragons, the best score.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): left/right move the gun, either button
' fires (one shot a press). On the title (START, HOW TO PLAY, CREDITS),
' up and down choose, a button
' chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

const TSt00 = 128
const TSt01 = 129
